Suite: Invoices API

Vocabulary:
  State: the invoice is none | draft | open | void
    Shows: draft: the invoice's `status` is `draft`
    Shows: open: the invoice's `status` is `open`
    Shows: void: the invoice's `status` is `void`
  State: the caller is admin | member
    # The API key a request is sent with decides who it is from.
    Set: use the {value}'s API key
  Event: the caller creates an invoice
    Say: create an invoice for the customer
  Event: the caller finalizes the invoice
    Say: finalize the invoice
  Event: the caller voids the invoice
    Say: void the invoice

  Phrase: a new account, as its admin
    Say: start on an account with no invoices, with the admin's API key
    Given the invoice is none
    And the caller is admin

Term: API key
  A secret that says which account a request is for and who in it is asking: an admin or a
  member. Send it as a bearer token in the `Authorization` header. The examples here read an
  admin's key from `$ADMIN_API_KEY`, a member's from `$MEMBER_API_KEY`, and the address of the
  API from `$BASE_URL`.

Background:
  Given a new account, as its admin

Feature: Invoices
  Approval: proposed

  An invoice is what your account bills a customer. You create it as a draft, which you can
  still change, then finalize it, which opens it for payment and gives it its number. Each
  request returns the invoice as it is now, and its `status` says where it is: `draft`, `open`
  or `void`.

  Every request is made with an [[API key]]. Admins and members of the account can both create
  and finalize invoices.

  Expectation: Creating an invoice makes a draft
    Given the invoice is none
    When the caller creates an invoice
    Then the invoice is draft

  Expectation: Finalizing a draft opens it
    Given the invoice is draft
    When the caller finalizes the invoice
    Then the invoice is open

Feature: Voiding
  Approval: proposed

  Void an open invoice that was issued by mistake: it stays on the account for the record, but
  can no longer be paid. Voiding can't be undone, so only an admin of the account can do it. A
  member who tries gets `403 Forbidden` and the invoice stays open.

  Expectation: Voiding an open invoice voids it
    Given the invoice is open
    When the caller voids the invoice
    Then the invoice is void

  Expectation: Members can't void
    Overrides: Voiding an open invoice voids it
    Given the invoice is open
    And the caller is member
    When the caller voids the invoice
    Then the invoice is open

Story: Bill a customer
  Approval: proposed
  As an admin of the account
  I want to issue an invoice, and take it back if it was a mistake
  So that my customer pays what they owe and nothing else

  You bill a customer in two requests: create the invoice as a draft, then finalize it. Until
  it is finalized the customer can't see it, so a draft is the place to get it right. The
  response to each request is the whole invoice, so you can read its `status` without asking
  again.

  If an open invoice turns out to be wrong, void it and issue a new one. A void invoice stays on
  the account, so the record shows what was sent.

  When the caller creates an invoice
  Then the invoice is draft
  When the caller finalizes the invoice
  Then the invoice is open
  When the caller voids the invoice
  Then the invoice is void

Story: Ask an admin to void
  Approval: proposed
  As a member of the account
  I want to bill customers without being able to void what was billed
  So that money can't quietly be taken back

  Members bill customers the same way admins do. Voiding is the one thing a member's
  [[API key]] can't do: the API answers `403 Forbidden` and leaves the invoice open, so ask an
  admin of the account to void it.

  Given the caller is member
  When the caller creates an invoice
  Then the invoice is draft
  When the caller finalizes the invoice
  Then the invoice is open
  When the caller voids the invoice
  Then the invoice is open
